# KNOWN DIVERGENCE — engine defect, unfixed. Found by DifferentialFuzzTest and
# shrunk automatically from 4000 operations down to these two.
#
# modifyOrder(id, newQty=0) is ACCEPTED (returns true, rejectReason=None) and
# leaves a ZERO-QUANTITY order resting in the book.
#
#   engine    : bid level 999800 -> totalQuantity=0, orderCount=1
#   reference : bid level 999800 -> totalQuantity=1, orderCount=1 (amendment refused)
#
# The engine already refuses an amendment that is not a reduction:
#   modifyOrder(id, same qty) -> false, RejectReason::InvalidQuantity
#   modifyOrder(id, larger)   -> false, RejectReason::InvalidQuantity
# Zero slips through that guard because 0 IS strictly less than the current
# quantity. Downstream consequences, all reproduced:
#   * the L2 feed publishes a price level with one order and zero size, and it
#     is the best bid — so the touch is a level that can never trade;
#   * validateIntegrity() still reports the book as consistent;
#   * the BookVisible feed emits Reduce(1) and never a delete, so an ITCH
#     consumer rebuilds the same phantom;
#   * an aggressor hitting that level produces a TRADE OF QUANTITY ZERO, and
#     the phantom's owner is told status=Filled filledQty=1 — a fill quantity
#     no trade ever carried, and one the counterparty's report contradicts.
#
# Whichever semantic was intended — refuse it, or treat it as a cancel — the
# engine does neither. This file is EXPECTED to diverge until that is fixed.
# Drop the ".known" from the filename once it is, so it becomes a hard
# regression case instead of a tracked defect.
A 1 30 2 B L 999800 1
M 1 30 * 0
